1
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:10,038
“Dear boy and Pip’s comrade. I am not a-going fur to tell you my life like a song, or a story-book.

2
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:17,199
But to give it you short and handy, I’ll put it at once into a mouthful of English. In jail and

3
00:00:17,199 --> 00:00:26,398
out of jail, in jail and out of jail, in jail and out of jail. There, you’ve got it. That’s

4
00:00:26,398 --> 00:00:32,639
my life pretty much, down to such times as I got shipped off, arter Pip stood my friend.

5
00:00:32,641 --> 00:00:37,519
“I’ve been done everything to, pretty well—except hanged. I’ve been locked up

6
00:00:37,519 --> 00:00:40,758
as much as a silver tea-kittle. I’ve been carted here and carted there,

7
00:00:40,758 --> 00:00:44,319
and put out of this town, and put out of that town, and stuck in the stocks,

8
00:00:44,319 --> 00:00:49,557
and whipped and worried and drove. I’ve no more notion where I was born than you have—if

9
00:00:49,559 --> 00:00:57,559
so much. I first become aware of myself down in Essex, a thieving turnips for my living. Summun

10
00:00:57,559 --> 00:01:04,237
had run away from me—a man—a tinker—and he’d took the fire with him, and left me wery cold.

11
00:01:04,237 --> 00:01:10,480
“I know’d my name to be Magwitch, chrisen’d Abel. How did I know it? Much as I know’d the

12
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:16,199
birds’ names in the hedges to be chaffinch, sparrer, thrush. I might have thought it

13
00:01:16,199 --> 00:01:22,480
was all lies together, only as the birds’ names come out true, I supposed mine did.

14
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:27,398
“So fur as I could find, there warn’t a soul that see young Abel Magwitch,

15
00:01:27,398 --> 00:01:32,877
with us little on him as in him, but wot caught fright at him, and either drove him off,

16
00:01:32,879 --> 00:01:39,917
or took him up. I was took up, took up, took up, to that extent that I reg’larly grow’d up took up.

17
00:01:41,398 --> 00:01:48,038
“This is the way it was, that when I was a ragged little creetur as much to be pitied as ever I see

18
00:01:48,038 --> 00:01:53,641
(not that I looked in the glass, for there warn’t many insides of furnished houses known to me),

19
00:01:53,641 --> 00:01:59,758
I got the name of being hardened. ‘This is a terrible hardened one,’ they says to prison

20
00:01:59,758 --> 00:02:06,480
wisitors, picking out me. ‘May be said to live in jails, this boy.’ Then they looked at me,

21
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:10,436
and I looked at them, and they measured my head, some on ’em,—they

22
00:02:10,438 --> 00:02:15,277
had better a measured my stomach,—and others on ’em giv me tracts what I couldn’t read, and

23
00:02:15,277 --> 00:02:21,557
made me speeches what I couldn’t understand. They always went on agen me about the Devil. But what

24
00:02:21,559 --> 00:02:27,597
the Devil was I to do? I must put something into my stomach, mustn’t I?—Howsomever, I’m a getting

25
00:02:27,597 --> 00:02:34,917
low, and I know what’s due. Dear boy and Pip’s comrade, don’t you be afeerd of me being low.

26
00:02:34,917 --> 00:02:40,519
“Tramping, begging, thieving, working sometimes when I could,—though that warn’t as often as you

27
00:02:40,519 --> 00:02:43,917
may think, till you put the question whether you would ha’ been over-ready to give me

28
00:02:43,917 --> 00:02:50,559
work yourselves,—a bit of a poacher, a bit of a labourer, a bit of a wagoner, a bit of a haymaker,

29
00:02:50,559 --> 00:02:57,957
a bit of a hawker, a bit of most things that don’t pay and lead to trouble, I got to be a man. A

30
00:02:57,957 --> 00:03:03,480
deserting soldier in a Traveller’s Rest, what lay hid up to the chin under a lot of taturs, learnt

31
00:03:03,480 --> 00:03:09,838
me to read; and a travelling Giant what signed his name at a penny a time learnt me to write.

32
00:03:10,641 --> 00:03:17,078
I warn’t locked up as often now as formerly, but I wore out my good share of key-metal still.

33
00:03:17,078 --> 00:03:22,160
“At Epsom races, a matter of over twenty years ago, I got acquainted wi’ a man whose skull

34
00:03:22,160 --> 00:03:28,319
I’d crack wi’ this poker, like the claw of a lobster, if I’d got it on this hob. His right

35
00:03:28,319 --> 00:03:34,519
name was Compeyson; and that’s the man, dear boy, what you see me a pounding in the ditch,

36
00:03:34,519 --> 00:03:39,758
according to what you truly told your comrade arter I was gone last night.

37
00:03:40,519 --> 00:03:46,319
“He set up fur a gentleman, this Compeyson, and he’d been to a public boarding-school and

38
00:03:46,319 --> 00:03:53,277
had learning. He was a smooth one to talk, and was a dab at the ways of gentlefolks.

39
00:03:53,277 --> 00:03:59,160
He was good-looking too. It was the night afore the great race, when I found him on the heath,

40
00:03:59,160 --> 00:04:05,319
in a booth that I know’d on. Him and some more was a sitting among the tables when I went in,

41
00:04:05,319 --> 00:04:10,237
and the landlord (which had a knowledge of me, and was a sporting one) called him out,

42
00:04:10,237 --> 00:04:16,557
and said, ‘I think this is a man that might suit you,’—meaning I was.

43
00:04:16,559 --> 00:04:21,160
“Compeyson, he looks at me very noticing, and I look at him. He

44
00:04:21,160 --> 00:04:27,917
has a watch and a chain and a ring and a breast-pin and a handsome suit of clothes.

45
00:04:27,917 --> 00:04:32,358
“‘To judge from appearances, you’re out of luck,’ says Compeyson to me.

46
00:04:32,358 --> 00:04:37,038
“‘Yes, master, and I’ve never been in it much.’ (I had come out of Kingston Jail last

47
00:04:37,038 --> 00:04:43,160
on a vagrancy committal. Not but what it might have been for something else; but it warn’t.)

48
00:04:43,160 --> 00:04:48,038
“‘Luck changes,’ says Compeyson; ‘perhaps yours is going to change.’

49
00:04:48,038 --> 00:04:51,839
“I says, ‘I hope it may be so. There’s room.’

50
00:04:51,839 --> 00:04:54,277
“‘What can you do?’ says Compeyson.

51
00:04:54,277 --> 00:04:58,358
“‘Eat and drink,’ I says; ‘if you’ll find the materials.’

52
00:04:58,358 --> 00:05:03,078
“Compeyson laughed, looked at me again very noticing, giv me five shillings,

53
00:05:03,078 --> 00:05:10,519
and appointed me for next night. Same place. “I went to Compeyson next night, same place,

54
00:05:10,519 --> 00:05:15,480
and Compeyson took me on to be his man and pardner. And what was Compeyson’s business

55
00:05:15,480 --> 00:05:21,237
in which we was to go pardners? Compeyson’s business was the swindling, handwriting forging,

56
00:05:21,237 --> 00:05:27,358
stolen bank-note passing, and such-like. All sorts of traps as Compeyson could set with his head,

57
00:05:27,358 --> 00:05:31,917
and keep his own legs out of and get the profits from and let another man in for,

58
00:05:31,917 --> 00:05:36,160
was Compeyson’s business. He’d no more heart than a iron file,

59
00:05:36,160 --> 00:05:41,480
he was as cold as death, and he had the head of the Devil afore mentioned.

60
00:05:41,480 --> 00:05:46,480
“There was another in with Compeyson, as was called Arthur,—not as being so chrisen’d,

61
00:05:46,480 --> 00:05:53,160
but as a surname. He was in a Decline, and was a shadow to look at. Him and Compeyson had been

62
00:05:53,160 --> 00:05:58,597
in a bad thing with a rich lady some years afore, and they’d made a pot of money by it;

63
00:05:58,597 --> 00:06:06,237
but Compeyson betted and gamed, and he’d have run through the king’s taxes. So, Arthur was a dying,

64
00:06:06,237 --> 00:06:14,237
and a dying poor and with the horrors on him, and Compeyson’s wife (which Compeyson kicked mostly)

65
00:06:14,237 --> 00:06:21,957
was a having pity on him when she could, and Compeyson was a having pity on nothing and nobody.

66
00:06:21,957 --> 00:06:27,228
“I might a took warning by Arthur, but I didn’t; and I won’t pretend I was partick’ler—for where

67
00:06:27,228 --> 00:06:32,917
’ud be the good on it, dear boy and comrade? So I begun wi’ Compeyson, and a poor tool I

68
00:06:32,917 --> 00:06:39,559
was in his hands. Arthur lived at the top of Compeyson’s house (over nigh Brentford it was),

69
00:06:40,237 --> 00:06:44,796
and Compeyson kept a careful account agen him for board and lodging, in case he should ever

70
00:06:44,796 --> 00:06:50,839
get better to work it out. But Arthur soon settled the account. The second or third

71
00:06:50,839 --> 00:06:56,078
time as ever I see him, he come a tearing down into Compeyson’s parlour late at night,

72
00:06:56,078 --> 00:07:02,879
in only a flannel gown, with his hair all in a sweat, and he says to Compeyson’s wife, ‘Sally,

73
00:07:02,879 --> 00:07:07,796
she really is upstairs alonger me, now, and I can’t get rid of her. She’s all in

74
00:07:07,796 --> 00:07:12,718
white,’ he says, ‘wi’ white flowers in her hair, and she’s awful mad, and she’s got

75
00:07:12,718 --> 00:07:18,000
a shroud hanging over her arm, and she says she’ll put it on me at five in the morning.’

76
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:22,438
“Says Compeyson: ‘Why, you fool, don’t you know she’s got a living body? And how should she be

77
00:07:22,438 --> 00:07:27,199
up there, without coming through the door, or in at the window, and up the stairs?’

78
00:07:27,199 --> 00:07:28,917
“‘I don’t know how she’s there,’ says Arthur,

79
00:07:28,917 --> 00:07:33,758
shivering dreadful with the horrors, ‘but she’s standing in the corner at the foot of the bed,

80
00:07:33,758 --> 00:07:39,519
awful mad. And over where her heart’s broke—you broke it!—there’s drops of blood.’

81
00:07:40,480 --> 00:07:45,917
“Compeyson spoke hardy, but he was always a coward. ‘Go up alonger this drivelling sick

82
00:07:45,917 --> 00:07:52,480
man,’ he says to his wife, ‘and Magwitch, lend her a hand, will you?’ But he never come nigh himself.

83
00:07:52,480 --> 00:07:57,237
“Compeyson’s wife and me took him up to bed agen, and he raved most dreadful. ‘Why look

84
00:07:57,237 --> 00:08:01,917
at her!’ he cries out. ‘She’s a shaking the shroud at me! Don’t you see her? Look

85
00:08:01,917 --> 00:08:08,078
at her eyes! Ain’t it awful to see her so mad?’ Next he cries, ‘She’ll put it on me,

86
00:08:08,078 --> 00:08:14,000
and then I’m done for! Take it away from her, take it away!’ And then he catched hold of us,

87
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:20,680
and kep on a talking to her, and answering of her, till I half believed I see her myself.

88
00:08:20,680 --> 00:08:26,160
“Compeyson’s wife, being used to him, giv him some liquor to get the horrors off, and by and by he

89
00:08:26,160 --> 00:08:34,116
quieted. ‘O, she’s gone! Has her keeper been for her?’ he says. ‘Yes,’ says Compeyson’s wife. ‘Did

90
00:08:34,116 --> 00:08:39,718
you tell him to lock her and bar her in?’ ‘Yes.’ ‘And to take that ugly thing away from her?’ ‘Yes,

91
00:08:39,718 --> 00:08:45,480
yes, all right.’ ‘You’re a good creetur,’ he says, ‘don’t leave me, whatever you do, and thank you!’

92
00:08:45,480 --> 00:08:48,957
“He rested pretty quiet till it might want a few minutes of five,

93
00:08:48,957 --> 00:08:54,480
and then he starts up with a scream, and screams out, ‘Here she is! She’s got the shroud again.

94
00:08:54,480 --> 00:08:59,038
She’s unfolding it. She’s coming out of the corner. She’s coming to the bed. Hold me,

95
00:08:59,038 --> 00:09:03,559
both on you—one of each side—don’t let her touch me with it. Hah! she missed

96
00:09:03,559 --> 00:09:07,879
me that time. Don’t let her throw it over my shoulders. Don’t let her lift me up to get it

97
00:09:07,879 --> 00:09:16,319
round me. She’s lifting me up. Keep me down!’ Then he lifted himself up hard, and was dead.

98
00:09:16,319 --> 00:09:21,557
“Compeyson took it easy as a good riddance for both sides. Him and me was soon busy,

99
00:09:21,559 --> 00:09:27,237
and first he swore me (being ever artful) on my own book,—this here little black book,

100
00:09:27,237 --> 00:09:30,957
dear boy, what I swore your comrade on.

101
00:09:30,957 --> 00:09:33,436
“Not to go into the things that Compeyson planned,

102
00:09:33,438 --> 00:09:39,559
and I done—which ’ud take a week—I’ll simply say to you, dear boy, and Pip’s comrade, that that

103
00:09:39,559 --> 00:09:45,641
man got me into such nets as made me his black slave. I was always in debt to him,

104
00:09:45,641 --> 00:09:52,277
always under his thumb, always a working, always a getting into danger. He was younger than me,

105
00:09:52,277 --> 00:09:57,597
but he’d got craft, and he’d got learning, and he overmatched me five hundred times told and

106
00:09:57,597 --> 00:10:03,917
no mercy. My Missis as I had the hard time wi’—Stop though! I ain’t brought her in—”

107
00:10:03,917 --> 00:10:09,319
He looked about him in a confused way, as if he had lost his place in the book of his remembrance;

108
00:10:09,957 --> 00:10:15,116
and he turned his face to the fire, and spread his hands broader on his knees,

109
00:10:15,116 --> 00:10:21,160
and lifted them off and put them on again. “There ain’t no need to go into it,” he said,

110
00:10:21,160 --> 00:10:27,436
looking round once more. “The time wi’ Compeyson was a’most as hard a time as ever I had;

111
00:10:27,438 --> 00:10:33,758
that said, all’s said. Did I tell you as I was tried, alone, for misdemeanor,

112
00:10:33,758 --> 00:10:38,438
while with Compeyson?” I answered, No.

113
00:10:38,438 --> 00:10:44,879
“Well!” he said, “I was, and got convicted. As to took up on suspicion, that was twice

114
00:10:44,879 --> 00:10:50,597
or three times in the four or five year that it lasted; but evidence was wanting. At last,

115
00:10:50,597 --> 00:10:55,038
me and Compeyson was both committed for felony,—on a charge of putting

116
00:10:55,038 --> 00:11:01,237
stolen notes in circulation,—and there was other charges behind. Compeyson says to me,

117
00:11:01,237 --> 00:11:08,678
‘Separate defences, no communication,’ and that was all. And I was so miserable poor,

118
00:11:08,680 --> 00:11:16,398
that I sold all the clothes I had, except what hung on my back, afore I could get Jaggers.

119
00:11:16,398 --> 00:11:21,480
“When we was put in the dock, I noticed first of all what a gentleman Compeyson looked,

120
00:11:21,480 --> 00:11:27,519
wi’ his curly hair and his black clothes and his white pocket-handkercher, and what a common sort

121
00:11:27,519 --> 00:11:33,438
of a wretch I looked. When the prosecution opened and the evidence was put short,

122
00:11:33,438 --> 00:11:39,239
aforehand, I noticed how heavy it all bore on me, and how light on him.

123
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:46,398
When the evidence was giv in the box, I noticed how it was always me that had come for’ard,

124
00:11:46,398 --> 00:11:52,877
and could be swore to, how it was always me that the money had been paid to, how it was

125
00:11:52,879 --> 00:11:58,160
always me that had seemed to work the thing and get the profit. But when the defence come on,

126
00:11:58,160 --> 00:12:03,680
then I see the plan plainer; for, says the counsellor for Compeyson, ‘My lord and gentlemen,

127
00:12:03,680 --> 00:12:09,239
here you has afore you, side by side, two persons as your eyes can separate wide;

128
00:12:10,078 --> 00:12:17,319
one, the younger, well brought up, who will be spoke to as such; one, the elder, ill brought up,

129
00:12:17,319 --> 00:12:23,796
who will be spoke to as such; one, the younger, seldom if ever seen in these here transactions,

130
00:12:23,796 --> 00:12:31,199
and only suspected; t’other, the elder, always seen in ’em and always wi’ his guilt brought home.

131
00:12:31,199 --> 00:12:36,796
Can you doubt, if there is but one in it, which is the one, and, if there is two in it, which is much

132
00:12:36,796 --> 00:12:43,559
the worst one?’ And such-like. And when it come to character, warn’t it Compeyson as had been to

133
00:12:43,559 --> 00:12:48,917
the school, and warn’t it his schoolfellows as was in this position and in that, and warn’t it

134
00:12:48,917 --> 00:12:55,796
him as had been know’d by witnesses in such clubs and societies, and nowt to his disadvantage? And

135
00:12:55,796 --> 00:13:01,718
warn’t it me as had been tried afore, and as had been know’d up hill and down dale in Bridewells

136
00:13:01,718 --> 00:13:07,839
and Lock-Ups! And when it come to speech-making, warn’t it Compeyson as could speak to ’em wi’

137
00:13:07,839 --> 00:13:12,160
his face dropping every now and then into his white pocket-handkercher,—ah! and wi’ verses

138
00:13:12,160 --> 00:13:17,639
in his speech, too,—and warn’t it me as could only say, ‘Gentlemen, this man at my side is a

139
00:13:17,641 --> 00:13:23,597
most precious rascal’? And when the verdict come, warn’t it Compeyson as was recommended to mercy

140
00:13:23,597 --> 00:13:28,758
on account of good character and bad company, and giving up all the information he could agen me,

141
00:13:28,758 --> 00:13:34,957
and warn’t it me as got never a word but Guilty? And when I says to Compeyson, ‘Once out of this

142
00:13:34,957 --> 00:13:41,597
court, I’ll smash that face of yourn!’ ain’t it Compeyson as prays the Judge to be protected, and

143
00:13:41,597 --> 00:13:48,160
gets two turnkeys stood betwixt us? And when we’re sentenced, ain’t it him as gets seven year, and me

144
00:13:48,160 --> 00:13:53,839
fourteen, and ain’t it him as the Judge is sorry for, because he might a done so well, and ain’t it

145
00:13:53,839 --> 00:14:00,917
me as the Judge perceives to be a old offender of wiolent passion, likely to come to worse?”

146
00:14:00,917 --> 00:14:04,957
He had worked himself into a state of great excitement, but he checked it,

147
00:14:04,957 --> 00:14:09,038
took two or three short breaths, swallowed as often, and stretching out his hand

148
00:14:09,559 --> 00:14:15,000
towards me said, in a reassuring manner, “I ain’t a-going to be low, dear boy!”

149
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:18,597
He had so heated himself that he took out his handkerchief and

150
00:14:18,597 --> 00:14:24,438
wiped his face and head and neck and hands, before he could go on.

151
00:14:24,438 --> 00:14:29,078
“I had said to Compeyson that I’d smash that face of his, and I swore Lord smash mine! to

152
00:14:29,078 --> 00:14:34,641
do it. We was in the same prison-ship, but I couldn’t get at him for long,

153
00:14:34,641 --> 00:14:39,641
though I tried. At last I come behind him and hit him on the cheek to turn him round and get

154
00:14:39,641 --> 00:14:45,758
a smashing one at him, when I was seen and seized. The black-hole of that ship warn’t a strong one,

155
00:14:45,758 --> 00:14:51,597
to a judge of black-holes that could swim and dive. I escaped to the shore, and I was

156
00:14:51,597 --> 00:14:58,319
a hiding among the graves there, envying them as was in ’em and all over, when I first see my boy!”

157
00:14:58,319 --> 00:15:03,398
He regarded me with a look of affection that made him almost abhorrent to me again,

158
00:15:03,398 --> 00:15:06,480
though I had felt great pity for him.

159
00:15:06,480 --> 00:15:12,597
“By my boy, I was giv to understand as Compeyson was out on them marshes too. Upon my soul,

160
00:15:12,597 --> 00:15:18,199
I half believe he escaped in his terror, to get quit of me, not knowing it was me as had got

161
00:15:18,199 --> 00:15:24,639
ashore. I hunted him down. I smashed his face. ‘And now,’ says I ‘as the worst thing I can do,

162
00:15:24,641 --> 00:15:29,597
caring nothing for myself, I’ll drag you back.’ And I’d have swum off,

163
00:15:29,597 --> 00:15:35,917
towing him by the hair, if it had come to that, and I’d a got him aboard without the soldiers.

164
00:15:35,917 --> 00:15:39,116
“Of course he’d much the best of it to the last,—his character

165
00:15:39,116 --> 00:15:46,038
was so good. He had escaped when he was made half wild by me and my murderous intentions;

166
00:15:46,038 --> 00:15:51,519
and his punishment was light. I was put in irons, brought to trial again,

167
00:15:51,519 --> 00:15:58,277
and sent for life. I didn’t stop for life, dear boy and Pip’s comrade, being here.”

168
00:15:58,277 --> 00:16:04,116
He wiped himself again, as he had done before, and then slowly took his tangle of tobacco from his

169
00:16:04,116 --> 00:16:10,917
pocket, and plucked his pipe from his button-hole, and slowly filled it, and began to smoke.

170
00:16:10,917 --> 00:16:13,796
“Is he dead?” I asked, after a silence.

171
00:16:13,796 --> 00:16:16,237
“Is who dead, dear boy?”

172
00:16:16,237 --> 00:16:17,957
“Compeyson.”

173
00:16:17,957 --> 00:16:19,519
“He hopes I am, if he’s alive,

174
00:16:19,519 --> 00:16:24,480
you may be sure,” with a fierce look. “I never heerd no more of him.”

175
00:16:24,480 --> 00:16:28,639
Herbert had been writing with his pencil in the cover of a book. He softly pushed

176
00:16:28,641 --> 00:16:34,398
the book over to me, as Provis stood smoking with his eyes on the fire, and I read in it:—

177
00:16:34,398 --> 00:16:37,557
“Young Havisham’s name was Arthur. Compeyson

178
00:16:37,559 --> 00:16:41,680
is the man who professed to be Miss Havisham’s lover.”

179
00:16:41,680 --> 00:16:45,957
I shut the book and nodded slightly to Herbert, and put the book by;

180
00:16:45,957 --> 00:16:50,116
but we neither of us said anything, and both looked at Provis as he stood smoking

